From 5bc046f28abf533fdc3880455088c585c91e7950 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 15:14:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 252/307] !B (Terrain Plate) Antialias bake footprints and
 match the weld curve.

Use supersampled cell coverage instead of a binary footprint edge and apply the
same smooth rim weight used by the mesh weld. The baked outline remains stable
across terrain LODs and follows the visible plate transition.
---
 .../Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.cpp        | 126 ++++++++++++++++--
 .../Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.h          |   6 +
 2 files changed, 121 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.cpp b/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.cpp
index bd82db5b..a7c4d00d 100644
--- a/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.cpp
+++ b/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.cpp
@@ -120,6 +120,52 @@ ICVar* g_pTerrainPlateBakeEpsilonCVar = nullptr;
 int    g_terrainPlateBakeSlopeCorrect = 1;
 ICVar* g_pTerrainPlateBakeSlopeCorrectCVar = nullptr;
 
+//! C1, decision 06: antialias the bake's PLAN VIEW footprint with the coverage the bake already
+//! computes. Without it ComputeBakeBlock decides inside / outside pointwise AT THE TERRAIN NODE, so a
+//! cell whose node is a millimetre outside the rotated plate keeps the whole baseline while its
+//! neighbour one unit away takes the whole relief: a straight plate edge that is not axis aligned with
+//! the heightmap grid bakes as a staircase of FULL relief amplitude. At LOD >= 1 the terrain mesh
+//! samples every 2^level units, so the staircase is amplified rather than smoothed, and that is the
+//! stair the user sees once the LOD-0 appended copy stops covering it.
+//! The supersample grid over the cell already counts how many of its samples land on the plate; using
+//! that ratio as a weight turns the hard in / out decision into a proportional one, i.e. the footprint
+//! becomes coverage instead of a boolean - which is what every terrain authoring tool does, and what
+//! CRYENGINE's own road falloff (RoadObject.cpp:534-546) is the template for. We took its cosine and
+//! stopped one step short of its coverage.
+//! Safe by construction with respect to decision 04 rule 1: the weight can only ever be SCALED DOWN, so
+//! a partly covered cell can only move back TOWARDS the baseline - it can never be lifted above the plate.
+//! The coverage estimate also needs a resolution: at the minimum of 2 samples per axis a cell has only
+//! 4 coverage levels, so the staircase would merely get four treads instead of two. 4 samples per axis,
+//! i.e. 16 levels, is the floor while this is on.
+//! It changes the GROUND, not the rendering, so it fixes the stair at every LOD and with
+//! e_TerrainIntegrateObjectsFull 0 as well.
+//! Escape hatch: e_TerrainPlateBakeEdgeAA 0 restores the pointwise test bit for bit (and the old sample
+//! count). It needs a rebake, which the cvar compare in PollBake performs by itself.
+int    g_terrainPlateBakeEdgeAA = 1;
+ICVar* g_pTerrainPlateBakeEdgeAACVar = nullptr;
+
+//! C2b, decision 06: make the bake's rim curve the same curve the mesh edge weld uses.
+//! The plate mesh is welded to the ground with weight = 1 - smoothstep(d / falloff) towards the sampled
+//! terrain (@see the edge weld block in the mesh generator), while the bake ramped the relief in with a
+//! cosine over the same band. Two different S curves over one band do not agree: the cosine runs up to
+//! about 1 % of the relief ABOVE the matching smoothstep in the inner half of the band, which on a plate
+//! with a few metres of relief is a few centimetres of terrain poking through the plate mesh - in
+//! patches, because which of the two wins also depends on the terrain quad twist. While the LOD-0
+//! appended copy exists it hides the fight; when the copy goes, that intersection is the band the user
+//! reports.
+//! With the curves matched the algebra closes. Writing w for the weld's pull weight and taking the
+//! baseline as the terrain the weld samples, meshZ - bakeZ = (plateZ - bakeZ) * (1 - w) - sink, and the
+//! first term is non negative wherever the plate is above the ground: the terrain is under the mesh
+//! everywhere in the band except inside the sink sub band.
+//! The 0.1 m Weld Sink is deliberately NOT mirrored into the bake. The sink exists precisely so that the
+//! outer ring of the plate ends BELOW the ground and no light leaks under the border; baking the same
+//! sink into the terrain would put the ring back onto the terrain surface and undo it. So the one place
+//! the terrain is above the mesh is the outer quarter of the band, by design, and it is bounded by Weld
+//! Sink - not the unbounded fraction of the relief the curve mismatch produced.
+//! Escape hatch: e_TerrainPlateBakeWeldCurve 0 restores the cosine.
+int    g_terrainPlateBakeWeldCurve = 1;
+ICVar* g_pTerrainPlateBakeWeldCurveCVar = nullptr;
+
 //! Bake trigger / bake apply tracing. The bake chain has a dozen places where it can legitimately do
 //! nothing (the property is off, the level's terrain is not in yet, the footprint is empty, the write
 //! was refused), and until this cvar existed every one of them was a silent return: the user ticked
@@ -208,6 +254,28 @@ void RegisterTerrainPlateCVars()
 	                                                     "0 - plain Min filter: conservative (it can never leave terrain above the plate) but it\n"
 	                                                     "    buries anything drawn on the baked terrain surface on a slope");
 
+	g_pTerrainPlateBakeEdgeAACVar = REGISTER_CVAR2("e_TerrainPlateBakeEdgeAA", &g_terrainPlateBakeEdgeAA, 1, VF_NULL,
+	                                               "Terrain Plate component with Bake Into Terrain = true: weight the baked relief of every\n"
+	                                               "terrain cell by how much of that cell the plate actually covers in plan, instead of\n"
+	                                               "deciding inside / outside at the cell node only. Without it a straight plate edge that is\n"
+	                                               "not aligned to the heightmap grid bakes as a one cell staircase carrying the FULL relief,\n"
+	                                               "which the coarser terrain LODs amplify rather than smooth.\n"
+	                                               "1 - default: the footprint is coverage, so the edge bakes as a one cell ramp that stays\n"
+	                                               "    straight in plan. Forces at least 4 x 4 bake samples per cell for the estimate\n"
+	                                               "0 - the pointwise test, bit for bit as before. Both settings need a rebake, which the\n"
+	                                               "    plate performs by itself when this cvar changes");
+
+	g_pTerrainPlateBakeWeldCurveCVar = REGISTER_CVAR2("e_TerrainPlateBakeWeldCurve", &g_terrainPlateBakeWeldCurve, 1, VF_NULL,
+	                                                  "Terrain Plate component with Bake Into Terrain = true: ramp the baked relief in over the\n"
+	                                                  "rim band with the same smoothstep the mesh edge weld uses, instead of a cosine. Two\n"
+	                                                  "different S curves over one band disagree by up to about 1 % of the relief, which is the\n"
+	                                                  "terrain poking through the plate mesh in patches inside the rim band once the LOD-0\n"
+	                                                  "appended copy stops covering it.\n"
+	                                                  "1 - default: bake and weld follow the same profile; the terrain stays under the mesh\n"
+	                                                  "    across the band, except inside the Weld Sink sub band where the border is buried\n"
+	                                                  "    on purpose\n"
+	                                                  "0 - the cosine falloff, as before");
+
 	g_pTerrainPlateDebugCVar = REGISTER_CVAR2("e_TerrainPlateDebug", &g_terrainPlateDebug, 0, VF_NULL,
 	                                          "Terrain Plate component: trace the bake trigger and the bake itself.\n"
 	                                          "0 - off\n"
@@ -238,6 +306,12 @@ void UnregisterTerrainPlateCVars()
 		if (g_pTerrainPlateBakeSlopeCorrectCVar != nullptr)
 			gEnv->pConsole->UnregisterVariable(g_pTerrainPlateBakeSlopeCorrectCVar->GetName());
 
+		if (g_pTerrainPlateBakeEdgeAACVar != nullptr)
+			gEnv->pConsole->UnregisterVariable(g_pTerrainPlateBakeEdgeAACVar->GetName());
+
+		if (g_pTerrainPlateBakeWeldCurveCVar != nullptr)
+			gEnv->pConsole->UnregisterVariable(g_pTerrainPlateBakeWeldCurveCVar->GetName());
+
 		if (g_pTerrainPlateDebugCVar != nullptr)
 			gEnv->pConsole->UnregisterVariable(g_pTerrainPlateDebugCVar->GetName());
 	}
@@ -247,6 +321,8 @@ void UnregisterTerrainPlateCVars()
 	g_pTerrainPlateBakeProbeSpacingCVar = nullptr;
 	g_pTerrainPlateBakeEpsilonCVar = nullptr;
 	g_pTerrainPlateBakeSlopeCorrectCVar = nullptr;
+	g_pTerrainPlateBakeEdgeAACVar = nullptr;
+	g_pTerrainPlateBakeWeldCurveCVar = nullptr;
 	g_pTerrainPlateDebugCVar = nullptr;
 }
 
@@ -2084,7 +2160,13 @@ void CTerrainPlateComponent::ComputeBakeBlock(const SBakeTerrainMetrics& metrics
 	const float meshSpacing = (m_lastGridX > 1 && m_lastGridY > 1)
 	                          ? min(worldScale.x / (float)(m_lastGridX - 1), worldScale.y / (float)(m_lastGridY - 1))
 	                          : metrics.unitSize;
-	const int   samplesPerAxis = clamp_tpl((int)(metrics.unitSize / max(0.0001f, meshSpacing)) + 1, 2, 6);
+	//
+	// C1 raises the floor from 2 to 4 while the edge antialiasing is on: the same grid is now also the
+	// coverage estimator, and 2 x 2 gives it only four levels - enough to turn a two tread staircase into
+	// a four tread one and no more. 4 x 4 is 16 levels, which is finer than the 12 bit height quantisation
+	// can carry anyway. It costs 16 SamplePlateSurface calls per cell at bake time and nothing at runtime.
+	const int   samplesPerAxisMin = (g_terrainPlateBakeEdgeAA != 0) ? 4 : 2;
+	const int   samplesPerAxis = clamp_tpl((int)(metrics.unitSize / max(0.0001f, meshSpacing)) + 1, samplesPerAxisMin, 6);
 	const float sampleStep = metrics.unitSize / (float)(samplesPerAxis - 1);
 
 	for (int ux = 0; ux < dim; ++ux)
@@ -2196,21 +2278,35 @@ void CTerrainPlateComponent::ComputeBakeBlock(const SBakeTerrainMetrics& metrics
 			// cell is never taken below the ground. The rim falloff below then blends the whole thing back
 			// to the baseline over the Weld Inner Falloff band, exactly as in Raise mode - so the border of
 			// the footprint is still the original ground, in both modes.
+			// How much of THIS CELL's footprint the plate covers in plan, from the same supersample grid the
+			// Min filter above already walked. It is a plan view measure, not a height one: a fully covered
+			// cell is 1, a cell straddling the rotated plate border is the fraction of its area inside.
+			const int   totalSamples = samplesPerAxis * samplesPerAxis;
+			const float coverage = (totalSamples > 0) ? clamp_tpl((float)samplesTaken / (float)totalSamples, 0.f, 1.f) : 0.f;
+
 			const float raised = max(baseline, plateSurface);
 			float combined = raised;
 
 			if (m_bakeMode == ETerrainPlateBakeMode::RaiseAndLower)
-			{
-				const int totalSamples = samplesPerAxis * samplesPerAxis;
-				const float coverage = (totalSamples > 0) ? clamp_tpl((float)samplesTaken / (float)totalSamples, 0.f, 1.f) : 0.f;
 				combined = raised + (plateSurface - raised) * coverage;
-			}
 
-			// Cosine falloff to the baseline over the rim, the curve CRYENGINE itself uses for the road
-			// falloff (RoadObject.cpp:631-635), so the border of the footprint is the baseline exactly.
+			// Falloff to the baseline over the rim band, so the border of the footprint is the baseline
+			// exactly. C2b: the curve is the mesh edge weld's, not a cosine. The weld pulls a vertex towards
+			// the ground with 1 - smoothstep(t), so the relief it leaves is smoothstep(t) - and that is the
+			// profile the bake has to follow if the two surfaces are not to cross inside the band.
+			// @see g_terrainPlateBakeWeldCurve for the algebra and for why Weld Sink is not mirrored here.
 			const float distanceToBorder = min(0.5f - fabs_tpl(localX), 0.5f - fabs_tpl(localY)) * 2.f;
 			const float t = clamp_tpl(distanceToBorder / band, 0.f, 1.f);
-			const float weight = (1.f - cosf(gf_PI * t)) * 0.5f;
+			float weight = (g_terrainPlateBakeWeldCurve != 0)
+			               ? (t * t * (3.f - 2.f * t))            // smoothstep(t) == 1 - the weld's 1 - smoothstep(t)
+			               : (1.f - cosf(gf_PI * t)) * 0.5f;      // the road falloff (RoadObject.cpp:631-635)
+
+			// C1: the plan view boundary is a coverage, not a boolean. Scaling the rim weight by it is the
+			// same operation the falloff already is - a blend back towards the baseline - so it inherits all
+			// of the falloff's guarantees, including that it can only ever move the cell DOWN under a raised
+			// plate and therefore never leaves terrain above the plate mesh (decision 04 rule 1).
+			if (g_terrainPlateBakeEdgeAA != 0)
+				weight *= coverage;
 
 			const float value = baseline + (combined - baseline) * weight;
 			const float delta = value - baseline;
@@ -2704,9 +2800,11 @@ void CTerrainPlateComponent::BuildBakeProbes(const SBakeTerrainMetrics& metrics)
 {
 	m_bakeProbes.clear();
 
-	// The two knobs this bake was computed with, so PollBake can tell when the console changes them.
+	// The knobs this bake was computed with, so PollBake can tell when the console changes them.
 	m_bakeEpsilonUsed = GetBakeEpsilonMeters();
 	m_bakeSlopeCorrectUsed = g_terrainPlateBakeSlopeCorrect;
+	m_bakeEdgeAAUsed = g_terrainPlateBakeEdgeAA;
+	m_bakeWeldCurveUsed = g_terrainPlateBakeWeldCurve;
 
 	const int dim = m_bakeRectSize + 1;
 	if ((int)m_bakeResult.size() != dim * dim)
@@ -2833,8 +2931,14 @@ void CTerrainPlateComponent::PollBake()
 
 	// Changing a bake cvar moves nothing in the terrain, so the probes below - which compare the terrain
 	// against the heights this plate last wrote - can never see it. Compare the values the live bake was
-	// computed with instead, so the two knobs can be A / B'd from the console without re-ticking Bake.
-	if (m_bakeEpsilonUsed != GetBakeEpsilonMeters() || m_bakeSlopeCorrectUsed != g_terrainPlateBakeSlopeCorrect)
+	// computed with instead, so the knobs can be A / B'd from the console without re-ticking Bake.
+	// The two rim shape knobs MUST be in this list: they change every height in the band, so a probe that
+	// did not know about them would read a terrain of the old shape, call it an external edit and repair
+	// the new shape away every poll.
+	if (m_bakeEpsilonUsed != GetBakeEpsilonMeters()
+	    || m_bakeSlopeCorrectUsed != g_terrainPlateBakeSlopeCorrect
+	    || m_bakeEdgeAAUsed != g_terrainPlateBakeEdgeAA
+	    || m_bakeWeldCurveUsed != g_terrainPlateBakeWeldCurve)
 	{
 		ReapplyBakeAfterExternalEdit(metrics);
 		return;
diff --git a/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.h b/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.h
index 927fc0a5..3d9d80c1 100644
--- a/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.h
+++ b/Code/CryPlugins/CryDefaultEntities/Module/DefaultComponents/Geometry/TerrainPlateComponent.h
@@ -536,6 +536,12 @@ protected:
 	//! change does not move the terrain, so the probes cannot see it; PollBake compares these instead.
 	float                    m_bakeEpsilonUsed = 0.f;
 	int                      m_bakeSlopeCorrectUsed = 0;
+	//! Same, for the two knobs that decide the SHAPE of the rim: e_TerrainPlateBakeEdgeAA and
+	//! e_TerrainPlateBakeWeldCurve. Both change every height the bake writes over the band, so without
+	//! this the probes would compare the terrain against a m_bakeResult of the other shape and repair it
+	//! back to the old one for ever - the B3 bug class.
+	int                      m_bakeEdgeAAUsed = 0;
+	int                      m_bakeWeldCurveUsed = 0;
 	//! A few quantisation steps of the coarsest touched sector: a height never survives a round trip
 	//! through SetTerrainElevation exactly.
 	float                    m_bakeProbeTolerance = 0.01f;
-- 
